    {
      "id": "overview",
      "title": "Overview",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Claude Debussy (1862–1918) broke from Germanic functional harmony toward colour, parallel motion, and modal scales — founder of musical Impressionism."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "key-works",
      "title": "Key works",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "*Prélude à l'après-midi d'un faune* (1894) treats chords as sonorities. *Clair de lune*, *La mer*, *Children's Corner*. Influenced film music and contemporary folk arranging."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "scales",
      "title": "Scales",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Whole-tone and pentatonic collections overlap with Celtic and Asian traditions. Avoids heavy V–I cadences — parallels modal Irish melody."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "voicing",
      "title": "Voicing",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Wide spacing, soft doublings, planing parallel triads. Colour when tunes 'float' without demanding classical cadences."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
